{"authored" [{"question" "q-104865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t)=16{t}^{3}+2$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t)=3{t}^{2}-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02cd66c0bff65dfb2b3294a95e880f7f48fe02286dbca5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-104865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{3}+2)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(48{t}^{5}-16{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[8{t}^{6}-4{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}-2t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(8-4+2-2)-0 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{2}-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{4}-6{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\frac{9{t}^{5}}{5}-2{t}^{3}+t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{5}-2+1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(\\frac{4}{5})}\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =5(3{t}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02c