e/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02cd66c0bff65dfb2b3294a95e880f7f48fe02286dbca5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02cd66c0bff65dfb2b3294a95e880f7f48fe02286dbca5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02cd66c0bff65dfb2b3294a95e880f7f48fe02286dbca5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02cd66c0bff65dfb2b3294a95e880f7f48fe02286dbca5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=3t$ and $g(t)=-3t+3$ with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02cd66c0bff65dfb2b3294a95e880f7f48fe02286dbca5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The distance between $f(t)=3t$ and $g(t)=-3t+3$ with respect to the given inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-g(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖3t-(-3t+3)‖ \\\\  & =‖6t-3‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨6t-3,6t-3⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨6t-3,6t-3⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{t}^{2}-36t+9)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[12{t}^{3}-18{t}^{2}+9t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(12-18+9)-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02cd66c0bff65dfb2b3294a95e880f7f48fe02286dbca5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d32d544f64288869856656ab96e293f08c8ae63ea6e9ba333a3d0621c986a343" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02cd66c0bff65dfb2b3294a95e880f7f48fe02286dbca5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02cd66c0bff65dfb2b3294a95e880f7f48fe02286dbca5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02cd66c0bff65dfb2b3294a95e880f7f48fe02286dbca5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02cd66c0bff65dfb2b3294a95e880f7f48fe02286dbca5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02cd66c0bff65dfb2b3294a95e880f7f48fe02286dbca5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{5}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02cd66c0bff65dfb2b3294a95e880f7f48fe02286dbca5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $2$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $p(t)=7{t}^{2}-t$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=4{t}^{2}-4t$ with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02cd66c0bff65dfb2b3294a95e880f7f48fe02286dbca5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{q(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)}{q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1)}\\cdot q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(7{t}^{2}-t){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t){|}_{t=i}}{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(4{t}^{2}-4t){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t){|}_{t=i}}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\cdot 8+0\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 0}{8\\cdot 8+0\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 0}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{64}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}-4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe distance between $p(t)=7{t}^{2}-t$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=4{t}^{2}-4t$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖p(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖7{t}^{2}-t-(4{t}^{2}-4t)‖ \\\\  & =‖3{t}^{2}+3t‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨3{t}^{2}+3t,3{t}^{2}+3t⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3{t}^{2}+3t,3{t}^{2}+3t⟩ & ={\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(3{t}^{2}+3t){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (3{t}^{2}+3t){|}_{t=i} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =0+0+36 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{36}=6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02cd66c0bff65dfb2b3294a95e880f7f48fe02286dbca5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d32d544f64288869856656ab96e293f08c8ae63ea6e9ba333a3d0621c986a343" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02cd66c0bff65dfb2b3294a95e880f7f48fe02286dbca5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02cd66c0bff65dfb2b3294a95e880f7f48fe02286dbca5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02cd66c0bff65dfb2b3294a95e880f7f48fe02286dbca5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02cd66c0bff65dfb2b3294a95e880f7f48fe02286dbca5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02cd66c0bff65dfb2b3294a95e880f7f48fe02286dbca5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02cd66c0bff65dfb2b3294a95e880f7f48fe02286dbca5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/state.json" "file_sha256" "72a4f4389fa4f7d9bc02cd66c0bff65dfb2b3294a95e880f7f48fe02286dbca5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V = \\Bbb R_1[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle p(t),q(t) \\rangle = p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $p(t)=-5t-3$ onto the subspace spanned by the polynomial $q(t) =t-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/example-8729.json" "file_sha256" "54fc162f1db27b39c5541e140bf8db32732bbfb79825fc8d07fe011f6ca3396a" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{ q(t) \\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) \n= \\dfrac{\\langle{p(t),q(t)}\\rangle}{\\langle{q(t),q(t)}\\rangle}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(-5t-3){|}_{0}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{0}+(-5t-3){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)+(-8)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3+0 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),q(t)⟩ & =q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(t-1){|}_{0}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{0}+(t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =3(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/example-8729.json" "file_sha256" "54fc162f1db27b39c5541e140bf8db32732bbfb79825fc8d07fe011f6ca3396a" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V = \\mathcal{C}[-1,1],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle =\\int_{-1}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf_1(t)=1,\\quad f_2(t)=\\sin \\pi t, \\quad g(t)=6t^2\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}\\{f_1(t), f_2(t)\\}$ given that $f_1(t) \\perp f_2(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_1(t)\\}$ equals $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/example-8730.json" "file_sha256" "59c9fbd969747e3bba1ff73978987beaf937af5047cd8246bd936d2c83ad3bb5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set $\\{f_1(t),f_2(t)\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,g(t) \n= \\textrm{proj}_{f_1(t)}\\,g(t) + \\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that $\\textrm{proj}_{f_1(t)}\\,g(t) = 2,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_2(t)\\}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t) \n= \\dfrac{\\langle{g(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle}{\\langle{f_2(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle}\\,f_2(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sin (\\pi t)$ is an odd function, while $6t^2$ is even. Hence, their product $6 t^2 \\sin (\\pi t)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-1,1],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1}   6t^2 \\sin (\\pi t) \\, \\text{d}t = 0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t) = \\dfrac{0}{\\langle{f_2(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle} \\cdot  \\sin \\pi t = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =2+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/example-8730.json" "file_sha256" "59c9fbd969747e3bba1ff73978987beaf937af5047cd8246bd936d2c83ad3bb5" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V = \\Bbb R_1[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle p(t), q(t) \\rangle = p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $p(t) = 4t + 2$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t) = t$ with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/example-8733.json" "file_sha256" "2c9df2ab7c7e01fb80d84eae3996f692eda064012ec6a9c86148ae59f85c18fa" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{ q(t) \\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)}{q(0)^{2}+q(1)^{2}}\\cdot q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)(0)+(6)(1)}{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\cdot t \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between $p(t) = 4t + 2$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t) = t$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖p(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖(4t+2)-6t‖ \\\\  & =‖-2t+2‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨-2t+2,\\text{ }-2t+2⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨-2t+2,\\text{ }-2t+2⟩ & =(-2t+2){|}_{0}\\cdot (-2t+2){|}_{0}+(-2t+2){|}_{1}\\cdot (-2t+2){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-2(0)+2)^{2}+(-2(1)+2)^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d = \\sqrt{4} = 2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/example-8733.json" "file_sha256" "2c9df2ab7c7e01fb80d84eae3996f692eda064012ec6a9c86148ae59f85c18fa" "basis" 1760 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14050792/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-104871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V={R}_{1}[t],$ the vector space of real polynomials of degree at most $1,$ and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1).\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t-1,\\,q(t)=4t-3\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t)}$ equals $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fb075f04df8a816645be1176e769c9e86c9029097be32c9c571c1d11169fa4d" "basis" 2198 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-104871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)=t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t)=\\frac{⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{p}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =q(0){p}_{2}(0)+q(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =(4t-3){|}_{0}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{0}+(4t-3){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={p}_{2}(0){p}_{2}(0)+{p}_{2}(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}{|}_{0}+(t-1)^{2}{|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) & =\\frac{3}{1}\\cdot (t-1)=3t-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =t+(3t-3) \\\\  & =4t-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fb075f04df8a816645be1176e769c9e86c9029097be32c9c571c1d11169fa4d" "basis" 2198 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-104871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "963a8ec6eafb257a900f118c020959eb09587853c6c1d8b22f9b17137d0e9345" "basis" 2198 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-104871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fb075f04df8a816645be1176e769c9e86c9029097be32c9c571c1d11169fa4d" "basis" 2198 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-104871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4t+3"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fb075f04df8a816645be1176e769c9e86c9029097be32c9c571c1d11169fa4d" "basis" 2198 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-104871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-8t+6"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fb075f04df8a816645be1176e769c9e86c9029097be32c9c571c1d11169fa4d" "basis" 2198 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-104871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fb075f04df8a816645be1176e769c9e86c9029097be32c9c571c1d11169fa4d" "basis" 2198 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-104871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "4t-3"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fb075f04df8a816645be1176e769c9e86c9029097be32c9c571c1d11169fa4d" "basis" 2198 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-104871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "8t-6"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fb075f04df8a816645be1176e769c9e86c9029097be32c9c571c1d11169fa4d" "basis" 2198 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-104871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4t-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fb075f04df8a816645be1176e769c9e86c9029097be32c9c571c1d11169fa4d" "basis" 2198 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $2$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $p(t)=5t$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=t-1$ with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fb075f04df8a816645be1176e769c9e86c9029097be32c9c571c1d11169fa4d" "basis" 2198 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{q(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)}{q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1)}\\cdot q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(5t){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{t=i}}{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(t-1){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{t=i}}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-5)\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 0}{(-2)\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =2(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe distance between $p(t)=5t$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=t-1$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖p(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖5t-2(t-1)‖ \\\\  & =‖3t+2‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨3t+2,3t+2⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3t+2,3t+2⟩ & ={\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(3t+2){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (3t+2){|}_{t=i} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2+5\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =1+4+25 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{30}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fb075f04df8a816645be1176e769c9e86c9029097be32c9c571c1d11169fa4d" "basis" 2198 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "963a8ec6eafb257a900f118c020959eb09587853c6c1d8b22f9b17137d0e9345" "basis" 2198 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fb075f04df8a816645be1176e769c9e86c9029097be32c9c571c1d11169fa4d" "basis" 2198 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}0"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fb075f04df8a816645be1176e769c9e86c9029097be32c9c571c1d11169fa4d" "basis" 2198 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{30}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fb075f04df8a816645be1176e769c9e86c9029097be32c9c571c1d11169fa4d" "basis" 2198 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{40}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fb075f04df8a816645be1176e769c9e86c9029097be32c9c571c1d11169fa4d" "basis" 2198 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{50}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fb075f04df8a816645be1176e769c9e86c9029097be32c9c571c1d11169fa4d" "basis" 2198 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{60}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fb075f04df8a816645be1176e769c9e86c9029097be32c9c571c1d11169fa4d" "basis" 2198 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{30}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/state.json" "file_sha256" "5fb075f04df8a816645be1176e769c9e86c9029097be32c9c571c1d11169fa4d" "basis" 2198 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066595/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-104927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $2$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $p(t)={t}^{2}+3t-2$ onto the subspace spanned by $q(t)=2t-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{q(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)=\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1) \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,+\\,3t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\cdot \\,(2t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=-1}\\,\\,\\,+\\,({t}^{2}\\,+\\,3t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=0}\\,\\,\\cdot \\,(2t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=0}\\,\\,\\,+\\,({t}^{2}\\,+\\,3t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=1}\\,\\,\\,\\cdot (2t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=1} \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot (-4)+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =16+4 \\\\  & =20,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),q(t)⟩ & =q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(2t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=-1}\\,\\,\\,\\cdot \\,(2t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=-1}\\,\\,\\,+(2t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=0}\\,\\,\\,\\cdot \\,(2t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=0}\\,\\,\\,+(2t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=1}\\,\\,\\,\\cdot \\,(2t\\,-\\,2){|}_{t=1} \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot (-4)+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =16+4+0 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{20}\\cdot (2t-2) \\\\  & =2t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54d5206748c7aa7d603332d0c7f6115a02fee15b2012d6c5279062ab9119351e"} {"question" "q-104927" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104927" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2-2t"] ["math" "2t-2"] ["math" "4-2t"] ["math" "4-4t"] ["math" "4t-4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104927" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2t-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t)=16{t}^{3}+2$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t)=3{t}^{2}-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{3}+2)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(48{t}^{5}-16{t}^{3}+6{t}^{2}-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[8{t}^{6}-4{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}-2t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(8-4+2-2)-0 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{t}^{2}-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{4}-6{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\frac{9{t}^{5}}{5}-2{t}^{3}+t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{5}-2+1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(\\frac{4}{5})}\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =5(3{t}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54d5206748c7aa7d603332d0c7f6115a02fee15b2012d6c5279062ab9119351e"} {"question" "q-104865" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104865" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3{t}^{2}"] ["math" "-5(3t-1)"] ["math" "5(3{t}^{2}-1)"] ["math" "9(3{t}^{2}-1)"] ["math" "{t}^{2}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104865" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5(3{t}^{2}-1)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V={R}_{1}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $p(t)=-3t-2$ onto the subspace spanned by the polynomial $q(t)=t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{q(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)=\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(-3t-2){|}_{0}\\cdot (t){|}_{0}+(-3t-2){|}_{1}\\cdot (t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 0+(-5)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0-5 \\\\  & =-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),q(t)⟩ & =q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(t){|}_{0}\\cdot (t){|}_{0}+(t){|}_{1}\\cdot (t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{1}\\cdot t \\\\  & =-5t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54d5206748c7aa7d603332d0c7f6115a02fee15b2012d6c5279062ab9119351e"} {"question" "q-104944" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104944" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4t"] ["math" "-5t"] ["math" "3t"] ["math" "4t"] ["math" "5t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104944" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-98694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t)=2{t}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t)=6t-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-98694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{t}^{2}\\cdot (6t-3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[3{t}^{4}-2{t}^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(3-2)-0 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{t}^{2}-36t+9)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[12{t}^{3}-18{t}^{2}+9t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(12-18+9)-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =2t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-98694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54d5206748c7aa7d603332d0c7f6115a02fee15b2012d6c5279062ab9119351e"} {"question" "q-98694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-98694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12t-6"] ["math" "2t-1"] ["math" "2{t}^{2}"] ["math" "6t-3"] ["math" "{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-98694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2t-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104936" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[-1,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[-1,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $f(t)={t}^{3}+10{t}^{2}+2$ onto the subspace spanned by $g(t)=5{t}^{2}+1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104936" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)=\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{3}+10{t}^{2}+2)\\cdot (5{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(5{t}^{5}+50{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}+20{t}^{2}+2)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{5}{6}{t}^{6}+10{t}^{5}+\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{4}+\\frac{20}{3}{t}^{3}+2t]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{6}+10+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{20}{3}+2)-(\\frac{5}{6}-10+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{20}{3}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{112}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),g(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(5{t}^{2}+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(25{t}^{4}+10{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[5{t}^{5}+\\frac{10}{3}{t}^{3}+t]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(5+\\frac{10}{3}+1)-(-5-\\frac{10}{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{112}{3})}{(\\frac{56}{3})}\\cdot (5{t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =2(5{t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =10{t}^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104936" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54d5206748c7aa7d603332d0c7f6115a02fee15b2012d6c5279062ab9119351e"} {"question" "q-104936" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104936" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-5{t}^{2}+2"] ["math" "-{t}^{2}+2"] ["math" "10{t}^{2}+2"] ["math" "5{t}^{2}+1"] ["math" "5{t}^{2}+2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104936" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10{t}^{2}+2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V={R}_{1}[t],$ the vector space of real polynomials of degree at most $1,$ and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1).\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=5t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=-2t+2,\\,q(t)=-t-4\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t)}$ equals $-5t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)=-5t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t)=\\frac{⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{p}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =q(0){p}_{2}(0)+q(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =(-t-4){|}_{0}\\cdot (-2t+2){|}_{0}+(-t-4){|}_{1}\\cdot (-2t+2){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 2+(-5)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={p}_{2}(0){p}_{2}(0)+{p}_{2}(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =(-2t+2)^{2}{|}_{0}+(-2t+2)^{2}{|}_{1} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) & =\\frac{-8}{4}\\cdot (-2t+2)=4t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =-5t+(4t-4) \\\\  & =-t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54d5206748c7aa7d603332d0c7f6115a02fee15b2012d6c5279062ab9119351e"} {"question" "q-105173" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105173" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4t+1"] ["math" "-t-4"] ["math" "4t-1"] ["math" "t+4"] ["math" "t-4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105173" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-t-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V={R}_{1}[t],$ the vector space of real polynomials of degree at most $1,$ and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1).\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t-1,\\,q(t)=4t-3\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t)}$ equals $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)=t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t)=\\frac{⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{p}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =q(0){p}_{2}(0)+q(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =(4t-3){|}_{0}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{0}+(4t-3){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={p}_{2}(0){p}_{2}(0)+{p}_{2}(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}{|}_{0}+(t-1)^{2}{|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) & =\\frac{3}{1}\\cdot (t-1)=3t-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =t+(3t-3) \\\\  & =4t-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54d5206748c7aa7d603332d0c7f6115a02fee15b2012d6c5279062ab9119351e"} {"question" "q-104871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4t+3"] ["math" "-8t+6"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4t-3"] ["math" "8t-6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-104871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4t-3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of real polynomials of degree at most $2,$ and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1).\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}+t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}-t,\\,q(t)=7t-3\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t)}$ equals $2{t}^{2}+2t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)=2{t}^{2}+2t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $q(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{p}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t)=\\frac{⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{p}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =q(-1){p}_{2}(-1)+q(0){p}_{2}(0)+q(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =(7t\\,-\\,3){|}_{-1}\\,\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{-1}+(7t\\,-\\,3){|}_{0}\\,\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{0}+(7t\\,-\\,3){|}_{1}\\,\\,\\cdot \\,({t}^{2}\\,-\\,t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-10)\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-20,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={p}_{2}(-1){p}_{2}(-1)+{p}_{2}(0){p}_{2}(0)+{p}_{2}(1){p}_{2}(1) \\\\  & =({t}^{2}-t)^{2}{|}_{-1}+({t}^{2}-t)^{2}{|}_{0}+({t}^{2}-t)^{2}{|}_{1} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) & =\\frac{-20}{4}\\cdot ({t}^{2}-t)=-5{t}^{2}+5t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,q(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{1}(t)}\\,q(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{p}_{2}(t)}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}+2t+(-5{t}^{2}+5t) \\\\  & =-3{t}^{2}+7t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54d5206748c7aa7d603332d0c7f6115a02fee15b2012d6c5279062ab9119351e"} {"question" "q-105411" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105411" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3{t}^{2}+7t"] ["math" "3{t}^{2}-7t"] ["math" "7{t}^{2}-3t"] ["math" "{t}^{2}+7t"] ["math" "{t}^{2}-7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105411" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3{t}^{2}+7t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[-1,1],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2},\\,g(t)=\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}$ equals $3t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=3t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)$ is an odd function, while ${t}^{2}$ is even. Hence, their product ${t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-1,1],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{0}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\cdot {t}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =3t+0 \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54d5206748c7aa7d603332d0c7f6115a02fee15b2012d6c5279062ab9119351e"} {"question" "q-105162" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105162" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{t}^{2}+3t"] ["math" "3t"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t"] ["math" "\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t+t"] ["math" "t\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105162" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=2,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}$ equals $6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=6,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+6{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is an odd function, while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is even. Hence, their product $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have integrals over the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) & =\\frac{-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =\\frac{-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =6-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54d5206748c7aa7d603332d0c7f6115a02fee15b2012d6c5279062ab9119351e"} {"question" "q-105186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "4+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "6-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "8+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $2$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $p(t)=5t$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=t-1$ with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{q(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)}{q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1)}\\cdot q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(5t){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{t=i}}{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(t-1){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{t=i}}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-5)\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 0}{(-2)\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =2(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe distance between $p(t)=5t$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=t-1$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖p(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖5t-2(t-1)‖ \\\\  & =‖3t+2‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨3t+2,3t+2⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3t+2,3t+2⟩ & ={\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(3t+2){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (3t+2){|}_{t=i} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2+5\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =1+4+25 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{30}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54d5206748c7aa7d603332d0c7f6115a02fee15b2012d6c5279062ab9119351e"} {"question" "q-105488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt{2}0"] ["math" "\\sqrt{30}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{40}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{50}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{60}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{30}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V={R}_{2}[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $2$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $p(t)=7{t}^{2}-t$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=4{t}^{2}-4t$ with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{q(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(-1)q(-1)+p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)}{q(-1)q(-1)+q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1)}\\cdot q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(7{t}^{2}-t){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t){|}_{t=i}}{{\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(4{t}^{2}-4t){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t){|}_{t=i}}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\cdot 8+0\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 0}{8\\cdot 8+0\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 0}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{64}\\cdot (4{t}^{2}-4t) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}-4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe distance between $p(t)=7{t}^{2}-t$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t)=4{t}^{2}-4t$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖p(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖7{t}^{2}-t-(4{t}^{2}-4t)‖ \\\\  & =‖3{t}^{2}+3t‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨3{t}^{2}+3t,3{t}^{2}+3t⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3{t}^{2}+3t,3{t}^{2}+3t⟩ & ={\\sum }_{i\\in {-1,0,1}}(3{t}^{2}+3t){|}_{t=i}\\cdot (3{t}^{2}+3t){|}_{t=i} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =0+0+36 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{36}=6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54d5206748c7aa7d603332d0c7f6115a02fee15b2012d6c5279062ab9119351e"} {"question" "q-105494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-98750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=2t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=6t-3$ with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-98750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}2t\\cdot (6t-3)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12{t}^{2}-6t)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{t}^{2}-36t+9)\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{[4{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}}{{[12{t}^{3}-18{t}^{2}+9t]}_{0}^{1}}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{(4-3)-0}{(12-18+9)-0}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =2t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between $f(t)=2t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=6t-3$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖2t-(2t-1)‖ \\\\  & =‖1‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨1,1⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1,1⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{1}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-98750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54d5206748c7aa7d603332d0c7f6115a02fee15b2012d6c5279062ab9119351e"} {"question" "q-98750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-98750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-98750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=3t$ and $g(t)=-3t+3$ with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The distance between $f(t)=3t$ and $g(t)=-3t+3$ with respect to the given inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-g(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖3t-(-3t+3)‖ \\\\  & =‖6t-3‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨6t-3,6t-3⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨6t-3,6t-3⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{t}^{2}-36t+9)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[12{t}^{3}-18{t}^{2}+9t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(12-18+9)-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54d5206748c7aa7d603332d0c7f6115a02fee15b2012d6c5279062ab9119351e"} {"question" "q-105461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105511" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=7t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=t-2$ with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105511" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}7t\\cdot (t-2)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(7{t}^{2}-14t)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-4t+4)\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{[\\frac{7}{3}{t}^{3}-7{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1}}{[\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+4t]_{0}^{1}}\\cdot (t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{14}{3})}{(\\frac{7}{3})}\\cdot (t-2) \\\\  & =-2t+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between $f(t)=7t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=t-2$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖7t-(-2t+4)‖ \\\\  & =‖9t-4‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨9t-4,9t-4⟩}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨9t-4,9t-4⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9t-4)\\cdot (9t-4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9t-4)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(81{t}^{2}-72t+16)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(27{t}^{3}-36{t}^{2}+16)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{7}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105511" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "54d5206748c7aa7d603332d0c7f6115a02fee15b2012d6c5279062ab9119351e"} {"question" "q-105511" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105511" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{6}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{7}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "q-105511" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{7}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/state.json" "file_sha256" "b06908f3e5a10ed8f761676230a773f72c208d49f4df7034ea675aded2b2bd14"} {"question" "e-8729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V = \\Bbb R_1[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle p(t),q(t) \\rangle = p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the orthogonal projection of $p(t)=-5t-3$ onto the subspace spanned by the polynomial $q(t) =t-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/example-8729.json" "file_sha256" "54fc162f1db27b39c5541e140bf8db32732bbfb79825fc8d07fe011f6ca3396a"} {"question" "e-8729" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{ q(t) \\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) \n= \\dfrac{\\langle{p(t),q(t)}\\rangle}{\\langle{q(t),q(t)}\\rangle}\\,q(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(-5t-3){|}_{0}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{0}+(-5t-3){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)+(-8)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3+0 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨q(t),q(t)⟩ & =q(0)q(0)+q(1)q(1) \\\\  & =(t-1){|}_{0}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{0}+(t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1}\\cdot (t-1) \\\\  & =3(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/example-8729.json" "file_sha256" "54fc162f1db27b39c5541e140bf8db32732bbfb79825fc8d07fe011f6ca3396a"} {"question" "e-8730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V = \\mathcal{C}[-1,1],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle =\\int_{-1}^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf_1(t)=1,\\quad f_2(t)=\\sin \\pi t, \\quad g(t)=6t^2\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}\\{f_1(t), f_2(t)\\}$ given that $f_1(t) \\perp f_2(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_1(t)\\}$ equals $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/example-8730.json" "file_sha256" "59c9fbd969747e3bba1ff73978987beaf937af5047cd8246bd936d2c83ad3bb5"} {"question" "e-8730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set $\\{f_1(t),f_2(t)\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,g(t) \n= \\textrm{proj}_{f_1(t)}\\,g(t) + \\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that $\\textrm{proj}_{f_1(t)}\\,g(t) = 2,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_2(t)\\}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t) \n= \\dfrac{\\langle{g(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle}{\\langle{f_2(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle}\\,f_2(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sin (\\pi t)$ is an odd function, while $6t^2$ is even. Hence, their product $6 t^2 \\sin (\\pi t)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-1,1],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1}   6t^2 \\sin (\\pi t) \\, \\text{d}t = 0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,g(t) = \\dfrac{0}{\\langle{f_2(t),f_2(t)}\\rangle} \\cdot  \\sin \\pi t = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =2+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/example-8730.json" "file_sha256" "59c9fbd969747e3bba1ff73978987beaf937af5047cd8246bd936d2c83ad3bb5"} {"question" "e-8733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $V = \\Bbb R_1[t],$ the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle p(t), q(t) \\rangle = p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $p(t) = 4t + 2$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t) = t$ with respect to the inner product." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/example-8733.json" "file_sha256" "2c9df2ab7c7e01fb80d84eae3996f692eda064012ec6a9c86148ae59f85c18fa"} {"question" "e-8733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The orthogonal projection of $p(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{ q(t) \\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t) & =\\frac{⟨p(t),q(t)⟩}{⟨q(t),q(t)⟩}\\,q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1)}{q(0)^{2}+q(1)^{2}}\\cdot q(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)(0)+(6)(1)}{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\cdot t \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between $p(t) = 4t + 2$ and the subspace spanned by $q(t) = t$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖p(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{q(t)}\\,p(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖(4t+2)-6t‖ \\\\  & =‖-2t+2‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨-2t+2,\\text{ }-2t+2⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨-2t+2,\\text{ }-2t+2⟩ & =(-2t+2){|}_{0}\\cdot (-2t+2){|}_{0}+(-2t+2){|}_{1}\\cdot (-2t+2){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-2(0)+2)^{2}+(-2(1)+2)^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d = \\sqrt{4} = 2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070180/example-8733.json" "file_sha256" "2c9df2ab7c7e01fb80d84eae3996f692eda064012ec6a9c86148ae59f85c18fa"}] "usage" {"e-8733" [] "q-105461" [] "q-105494" [] "e-8730" [] "q-105162" [] "e-8729" [] "q-105488" [] "q-104865" [] "q-98750" [] "q-104944" [] "q-104871" [] "q-105173" [] "q-98694" []} "basis" 2451 "retractions" [[17592186133982 :question/problem "Let $V = R_{1}[t]$, the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $1$ with real coefficients. Given that the inner product on $V$ is de